messages:
emily: Heads-up — I opened a docs PR to fix a few SDK snippets for streaming + pagination (Python/TS/Go). The current examples cause resource leaks under backpressure and the Go example doesn't cancel the goroutine. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/489

sam: thanks! can you list the exact breakage patterns so i can triage the SDK side?

emily: sure — three things i hit in manual tests:
1) Python streaming example uses a naive for loop over the async iterator but never exits early on downstream cancel, so the generator keeps pulling tokens.
2) TS snippet forgot to await the async iterator close in an error path (leaked open connection).
3) Go example spawns a goroutine for streaming but never signals ctx.Done() correctly; also missing retry/backoff guidance for pagination endpoints.

raj: for Python i'd suggest showing both a simple try/except/finally pattern and a contextmanager-style helper. example to add:
```python
# safe_stream.py (snippet)
async def stream_with_cancel(stream):
    try:
        async for chunk in stream:
            yield chunk
    except asyncio.CancelledError:
        # ensure underlying connection is closed cleanly
        await stream.aclose()
        raise
    finally:
        if not stream.closed:
            await stream.aclose()
```

sam: that looks good. for ts i can add a small wrapper that ensures .return() is called on the async iterator on error:
```ts
async function drainSafely(it) {
  try {
    for await (const p of it) {
      yield p;
    }
  } catch (e) {
    if (it.return) await it.return();
    throw e;
  }
}
```

mike: for Go i can update the example to use context cancellation and a select in the receive loop, plus a small retry helper for paginated endpoints. snippet:
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 30*time.Second)
defer cancel()
stream, err := client.Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return err }
for {
  select {
  case <-ctx.Done():
    stream.CloseSend()
    return ctx.Err()
  default:
    msg, err := stream.Recv()
    if err == io.EOF { break }
    if err != nil { return err }
    process(msg)
  }
}
```

lina: also suggested adding a short blurb in the pagination guide about exponential backoff when fetching pages (avoid tight retry loops) and an example backoff function we can reference across SDKs.

emily: perfect. can we also add a tiny test harness in docs that runs the snippets (smoke test) so we catch regressions? maybe a GH action that runs the examples in Python/TS/Go quickly.

